Module wrayford "The Third Night" {
  // ── Endings ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Reached at the captain's verdict on the fourth morning, by what the three days prepared. The first that holds is
  // the one told. None of them is a custom kept for ever after: each is the morning after, told plainly.
  Ending fire "The Fire" when verdictRebellion(wray) "The captain gives her verdict at nine in the Guildhall, and it takes one sentence. By noon the carters' row is burning, Wain Row and the yards behind it, and the captain's riders stand at the end of every lane to see that nobody puts it out. Ten men are hanged from the gatehouse rail beside the three, which is the lord's law for a rising, and the rail is not long enough, and they use the drawbridge chains. In the evening the riders go home down the south road with the toll money and the lord's thanks, and Wrayford stands in its own smoke and does not speak to itself for a generation. The Company rebuilds its counting-house in stone.".
  Ending too_late "Too Late" when verdictGiven(wray) and tooLate(wray) "The captain hangs Maudie Pardoe from the gatehouse rail at nine in the morning, beside her husband, under the reeve's own decree, which is on the door in his son's best hand. Simon Hulme stands below with his hat in his hands and his knife still in his coat, where it was when he came down the Cartway a quarter of an hour behind her. He resigns that afternoon. In a week he is gone from Wrayford, nobody knows where. The three are taken down at noon and buried with her in Wade's Acre, four graves by the church door, and old Corbin digs them, and says nothing at all, and the reeve's house on the Hill stands empty, and nobody in the city will buy it.".
  Ending away "Away" when verdictGiven(wray) and maudieHanged(maudie) and not inWrayford() "You are not in Wrayford when it happens, and you hear it weeks later, in another country, from a drover in an inn, the way news of a place you have left always comes: badly told, by someone who does not know they are telling it to you. A carter's widow went up to the gatehouse in the night with her bread knife to cut her husband down, and the captain hanged her beside him in the morning, under the reeve's own decree. The drover shakes his head and says it was a hard thing, and drinks, and talks about the price of cattle.".
  Ending third_night "The Third Night" when verdictGiven(wray) and maudieHanged(maudie) "At nine in the morning Captain Sefton gives her verdict in the Guildhall: Wrayford is in order, its ringleaders on the rail, and one more, taken in the act against the lord's dead, under the reeve's decree. She hangs Maudie Pardoe from the gatehouse rail beside her husband before ten. The reeve watches from the Bridge End and does not look away, because he wrote it, or his son did. The captain takes the toll money and goes home down the south road, and at dusk on the fourth day the reeve has all four cut down and given to the Abbess, and Corbin digs, and Wrayford buries them by the church door; and every year after, on the third night, nobody in Northgate goes out of doors.".
  Ending bargain "The Company's Bargain" when verdictGiven(wray) and kitSold(kit) "The captain takes Kit Pardoe south with her at noon, tied to a rider's saddle, for the lord's justice at Harbury, which is a rope. The Company, as it promised, asks the captain's mercy for the dead, justice having been done, and she grants it with a look at Alderman Gidley that he will not forget, and the three are buried that afternoon in Wade's Acre. Maudie Pardoe stands at the graveside and does not look at you once. You have forty crowns. The Company prints a ballad of the son who let his father die for him, at a penny, and it sells well.".
  Ending mothers "The Two Mothers" when verdictGiven(wray) and mothersWalked(wray) "When the captain comes up the south road at dusk on the third day there is nothing on the rail. There is a funeral: four new graves by the church door in Wade's Acre, the toll-keeper's boy among them, and the whole of Wrayford still standing about in the Liberty in its black, not going home. She looks at it a long time from her horse. In the morning she gives her verdict in one line: that the city is in order. She takes the toll money and goes home. The toll stands; the Company keeps its farm; Simon Hulme is not reeve any more. On Bridge Street, in the toll-keeper's house, Jennet Hobday has opened her shutters, and Maudie Pardoe comes on Sundays, with bread.".
  Ending charity "The Liberty's Charity" when verdictGiven(wray) and releasedBy(wray, by_charity) "The captain finds nothing on the rail but three cut ropes, and a letter from the Abbess claiming her dead, and the Bench's clause in Inchbold's copperplate, and the collector's ruling, and three new graves in Wade's Acre, dug square by old Corbin, who says it is the best work he has done in forty years. She reads the papers at supper with an expression nobody can read. In the morning she gives her verdict, that Wrayford is in order, and goes home with the toll money. The reeve keeps his office. The Company keeps its farm, and has its charity entered in its minutes, at the Liberty's charge. Everyone in Wrayford is a little ashamed, and the rail is empty, and on the whole it is a better morning than the city deserved.".
  Ending reeve "The Reeve's Release" when verdictGiven(wray) and reeveRelease(wray) "The captain hears at supper that the reeve of Wrayford cut the rebels down from the lord's rail with his own hands. In the morning she gives her verdict: that the city is in order, and its reeve is not. Simon Hulme hands her his chain of office on the Guildhall steps, and she takes it, not unkindly, and rides home with it and the toll money. He goes on living in the house on the Hill, and keeps a garden, and on the third night of every month walks down to the Bridge End and stands under the gatehouse for an hour, and nobody asks him why.".
  Ending earth "Earth on Him" when verdictGiven(wray) and buriedByNight(wray) "When the captain comes up the south road there is nothing on the rail but three cut ropes, and in Wade's Acre there are three graves nobody will admit to digging. The reeve has his answer for her, because he had to have one. She takes it, and the toll money, and gives her verdict that Wrayford is in order, and goes. In Wain Row Maudie Pardoe bakes bread for three days and gives it all away. Nobody in the city ever says, out loud, who climbed the gatehouse in the small hours, and everybody knows.".
  Ending third_man "The Third Man" when verdictGiven(wray) and (buried(wat_dead) or buried(dunstan_dead) or buried(joss_dead)) and not buriedByNight(wray) and not buriedLawfully(wray) "When the captain comes up the south road there is nothing on the rail but three cut ropes, and in Wade's Acre there are new graves that nobody will admit to digging, and not enough of them. The one who could not be got over the water before the river rose was found by the reeve's men at first light, where he was left, and the captain has him put in the lord's lime at the crossroads on the south road, alone, which is the lord's law for a rebel's body. Then she gives her verdict that Wrayford is in order, and goes. Ever after, in the alehouses of Northgate, Wrayford argues about which of the three it was, and whether it matters; and it does.".
  Ending lime "The Lime" when verdictGiven(wray) and not buried(wat_dead) and not buried(dunstan_dead) and not buried(joss_dead) and not maudieHanged(maudie) "The captain gives her verdict that Wrayford is in order, and orders the ringleaders taken down and put in the lord's lime at the crossroads on the south road, which is the lord's law for rebels' bodies. It is done at noon. There is no grave. Maudie Pardoe stands in her doorway in Wain Row while the cart goes past to the south road with the three in it, and does not go in until it is out of sight, and does not speak to you, or to anyone, for a long time; and Corbin, at the Acre, picks up his spade and digs for Ned Hobday and for Gidley's aunt, very slowly, because there is no reason now not to.".

  // ── What became of them, told at the verdict ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Rule fate_kit_hanged when verdictGiven(wray) and kitHanged(kit) and not kitSold(kit) and not spread(fate_kit) says "Kit Pardoe goes south with the captain, tied to a rider's saddle, for the lord's justice at Harbury. He looks back once, at the bridge." { Assert spread(fate_kit). }
  Entity fate_kit: fact "Kit's fate".
  Rule fate_maudie_fire when verdictGiven(wray) and verdictRebellion(wray) and maudieHanged(maudie) and not spread(fate_maudie) says "Maudie Pardoe is the first of the ten. She goes up the gate-ward's stair on her own feet, and does not look for anyone in the crowd." { Assert spread(fate_maudie). }
  Entity fate_maudie: fact "Maudie's fate".
  Rule fate_kit_free when verdictGiven(wray) and not kitTaken(kit) and not spread(fate_kit) and kitTrust(kit) >= 2 says "Kit Pardoe goes back to the yards, and to his father's place on the box, and the horse that went forward in the press, which he will not let anyone else drive." { Assert spread(fate_kit). }
  Rule fate_kit_cellar when verdictGiven(wray) and kitTaken(kit) and not kitHanged(kit) and not spread(fate_kit) says "The captain, having what she came for, has Kit Pardoe let out of the cellar with a cuff and a warning, and he walks home up the Cartway in the sun with his hands in his pockets." { Assert spread(fate_kit). Retract kitTaken(kit). Assert at(kit, carters_yards). }
  Rule fate_hal when verdictGiven(wray) and halTakesBlame(hal) and not spread(fate_hal) says "Hal Hulme comes out of the cellar when the captain has gone, and stands blinking in the Market, and his father is waiting for him at the Guildhall door, and they go home together up the Mercer Steps, very slowly, not talking." { Assert spread(fate_hal). }
  Entity fate_hal: fact "Hal's fate".
  Rule fate_hal_told_cut when verdictGiven(wray) and halToldFather(hal) and reeveRelease(wray) and not halTakesBlame(hal) and not spread(fate_hal) says "Hal Hulme, it is said, told his father what he did on the night of the rising, and his father sat with his head in his hands until dawn; and it was the next day that the reeve went down to the Bridge End with his knife." { Assert spread(fate_hal). }
  Rule fate_hal_told when verdictGiven(wray) and halToldFather(hal) and not reeveRelease(wray) and not halTakesBlame(hal) and not spread(fate_hal) says "Hal Hulme, it is said, told his father what he did on the night of the rising, before supper, and his father sat with his head in his hands until dawn, and in the morning went to his court as usual." { Assert spread(fate_hal). }
  Rule fate_corbin when verdictGiven(wray) and reconciled(wray) and not spread(fate_corbin) says "At the end of the week the banns are read at Saint Wade's for Corbin, sexton, of this parish, and Dorcas Mudd, ferry-woman, of the city, and the whole Liberty laughs, and so does the whole Wharf, and neither of them minds at all." { Assert spread(fate_corbin). }
  Entity fate_corbin: fact "Corbin and Dorcas".
  Rule fate_annis when verdictGiven(wray) and annisRing(annis) and not spread(fate_annis) says "Annis Pye wears Joss Wilder's ring on a string round her neck, and does not set up her pitch at the Bridge End again, but on the Wharf, where nobody ever hanged anybody." { Assert spread(fate_annis). }
  Entity fate_annis: fact "Annis".
  Rule fate_robin when verdictGiven(wray) and (robinHome(robin) or robinStays(robin)) and buried(wat_dead) and not spread(fate_robin) says "At the burying, somebody's boy beat a drum, slow, a bear-ward's drum with a bear painted on it: Robin Tull, who had nobody to bury, and came anyway." { Assert spread(fate_robin). }
  Entity fate_robin: fact "Robin".
  Rule fate_win when verdictGiven(wray) and winHelped(party) and buried(dunstan_dead) and not spread(fate_win) says "Win Fenner stood at Dunstan's grave with her four children in a row by height, and the chisels in the eldest's hands." { Assert spread(fate_win). }
  Entity fate_win: fact "Win".
  Rule fate_pinch when verdictGiven(wray) and (captainPinch(party) or pinchExposed(wray)) and not pinchGone(pinch) and not spread(fate_pinch) says "Amos Pinch goes south with the captain's baggage, for the lord's court at Harbury, on foot behind the led horse, with the day-book under his arm. He asked to carry it himself." { Assert spread(fate_pinch). Assert pinchGone(pinch). Assert at(pinch, limbo). }
  Entity fate_pinch: fact "Pinch".
}
